,"code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"intransitive: to conceive a crime or misdeed","word":"tramar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to trace out a graph or diagram","word":"traçar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to mark a point on a graph","word":"marcar"}],"pos":"verb","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"They had plotted a robbery.","bold_text_offsets":[[9,16]],"type":"example","id":"7debb3b2-3320-4042-9cde-076d8f19e7ac"},{"text":"They were plotting against the king.","bold_text_offsets":[[10,18]],"type":"example","id":"5a86cc21-9796-4a92-8f8a-79a2f2546175"}],"categories":["English intransitive verbs","English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs"],"links":[["conceive","conceive"],["crime","crime"],["misdeed","misdeed"]],"raw_glosses":["(transitive, intransitive) To conceive (a crime, misdeed etc)."],"glosses":["To conceive (a crime, misdeed etc)."],"tags":["intransitive","transitive"],"id":"3fb11035-fe95-4f68-87f5-e7682ffcefbe"},{"examples":[{"text":"They plotted the number of edits per day.","bold_text_offsets":[[5,12]],"type":"example","id":"1e181d99-94ae-4118-83b3-a7a7ef16cc5a"}],"categories":["English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs"],"raw_glosses":["(transitive) To trace out (a graph or diagram)."],"glosses":["To trace out (a graph or diagram)."],"tags":["transitive"],"id":"054a60c1-8e6f-44b7-a1c7-6ab6efe35098"},{"examples":[{"text":"Every five minutes they plotted their position.","bold_text_offsets":[[24,31]],"type":"example","id":"0ae8d298-3555-4223-9547-8f1b9dc91ab2"},{"text":"This treatise plotteth down Cornwall as it now standeth.","ref":"1602, Richard Carew, Survey on Cornwall:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[14,22]],"id":"25434f5c-24bb-4191-8d2b-7f48369fc076"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs","Quotation templates to be cleaned"],"raw_glosses":["(transitive) To mark (a point on a graph, chart, etc)."],"glosses":["To mark (a point on a graph, chart, etc)."],"tags":["transitive"],"id":"ec070e74-1c09-4bfb-8036-29aa5fc64dec"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[{"sense":"contrive","word":"becast"},{"sense":"conceive a crime, etc","word":"connive"},{"sense":"an area of land","word":"lot"}]},"plough":{"id":"05c6a5ad-a647-4131-8eb1-e2b4051be358","word":"plough","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"use a plough on soil to prepare for planting","word":"arar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"use a plough on soil to prepare for planting","word":"lavrar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"use a plough","word":"arar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"use a plough","word":"lavrar"}],"pos":"verb","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"I've still got to plough that field.","bold_text_offsets":[[18,24]],"type":"example","id":"36a8415b-1478-493c-86ba-a232e2c1b7b3"},{"text":"That there is and from time immemorial has been within that part of the parish called Mablethorpe St. Mary's a laudable custom that, if any outdweller take ancient pasture ground, he shall pay a modus of 4d. an acre, and so in proportion, on the 1st of August, in lieu of all manner of tithe; and that if any of the ancient pasture be once ploughed up or meadowed, it shall, when restored to pasture again, pay 4d. the acre in the hands of such outdweller.","ref":"1917, The English Reports: Exchequer, page 789:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[340,348]],"id":"08266ef0-b600-4a73-bc6d-c923c3fad527"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs"],"raw_glosses":["(transitive) To use a plough on soil to prepare for planting."],"glosses":["To use a plough on soil to prepare for planting."],"tags":["transitive"],"id":"e30c1e6b-8712-48eb-81a6-a4e60c0ac0b9"},{"examples":[{"text":"Some days I have to plough from sunrise to sunset.","bold_text_offsets":[[20,26]],"type":"example","id":"efd26db4-b853-4a5a-8df3-3fed78729f23"}],"categories":["English intransitive verbs","English terms with usage examples"],"raw_glosses":["(intransitive) To use a plough."],"glosses":["To use a plough."],"tags":["intransitive"],"id":"2d9f2408-60e9-44bd-93c2-833eccce28b9"},{"examples":[{"text":"Trucks ploughed through the water to ferry flood victims to safety.","bold_text_offsets":[[7,15]],"type":"example","id":"1bfefba8-8dcf-4c6c-89a7-b38ec6021b72"},{"text":"Wolves continued to plough forward as young Belgian midfielder Mujangi Bia and Ronald Zubar both hit shots wide from good positions.","ref":"2011 January 18, “Wolverhampton 5 - 0 Doncaster”, in BBC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[20,26]],"id":"32ee03fa-67fa-4e34-b056-774fe338df57"},{"text":"Thirteen people were injured in August 1957 when this Bristol freighter skidded on the runway at Southend Airport when landing with a flight from Calais. It ploughed through the boundary fence, but thankfully stopped short of the railway and the 1,500V overhead wires. A tripwire was installed on this section of Shenfield-Southend line to warn train drivers of instances such as this.","ref":"2020 December 30, Tim Dunn, “The railway's mechanical marvels”, in Rail, page 58, photo caption:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[157,165]],"id":"4341f486-fea6-4f00-a99d-50429b451041"}],"links":[["move","move"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples"],"glosses":["To move with force."],"id":"c86c542a-ab1f-4908-944e-04d1ab4b666c"},{"examples":[{"text":"My brother ploughed me over.","bold_text_offsets":[[11,19]],"type":"example","id":"68489e6b-ca67-4452-9501-ac3c6c02a607"},{"text":"Three people were ploughed down when he lost control of the truck.","bold_text_offsets":[[18,26]],"type":"example","id":"56900d33-c782-4ac9-bf35-67b0587ccee2"}],"categories":["English colloquialisms","English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs"],"links":[["knock over","knock over"],["run over","run over"]],"raw_glosses":["(transitive, colloquial) To knock over or run over (someone) without stopping."],"glosses":["To knock over or run over (someone) without stopping."],"tags":["colloquial","transitive"],"id":"3c0b5516-4443-4728-ac18-dffa83a8832c"},{"examples":[{"text":"Let patient Octavia plough thy visage up / With her prepared nails.","ref":"c. 1606–1607 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Tragedie of Anthonie and Cleopatra”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act IV, scene xii]:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[20,26]],"id":"3553858f-8787-4a18-856a-a1148338f84a"}],"links":[["furrow","furrow"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"chamfer"},{"word":"groove"},{"word":"rut"}],"glosses":["To furrow; to make furrows, grooves, or ridges in."],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"id":"e062f2c2-a30f-4389-9469-60a5afc0a709"},{"examples":[{"text":"With speed we plough the watery way.","ref":"1725, Homer, “Book II”, in [William Broome], transl., The Odyssey of Homer. […], volume I, London: […] Bernard Lintot, →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[14,20]],"id":"f18f840c-62e3-476b-9725-9a0902203544"}],"topics":["nautical","transport"],"categories":["English terms with quotations","en:Nautical"],"links":[["nautical","nautical"]],"raw_glosses":["(nautical) To run through, as in sailing."],"glosses":["To run through, as in sailing."],"id":"4b3f0a80-90a7-45ca-afc8-7ca2de20e624"},{"topics":["arts","bookbinding","crafts","hobbies","lifestyle"],"categories":["en:Bookbinding"],"links":[["bookbinding","bookbinding"],["trim","trim"]],"raw_glosses":["(bookbinding) To trim, or shave off the edges of, as a book or paper, with a plough."],"glosses":["To trim, or shave off the edges of, as a book or paper, with a plough."],"id":"7018ec5a-bb97-4b63-bcc0-0d7ba7e34230"},{"links":[["groove","groove"],["plank","plank"],["board","board"]],"raw_glosses":["(joinery) To cut a groove in, as in a plank, or the edge of a board; especially, a rectangular groove to receive the end of a shelf or tread, the edge of a panel, a tongue, etc."],"glosses":["To cut a groove in, as in a plank, or the edge of a board; especially, a rectangular groove to receive the end of a shelf or tread, the edge of a panel, a tongue, etc."],"id":"c99cedff-84ee-425c-8a7d-fc38ce9a5fc1"},{"examples":[{"text":"The good Professor scolded, predicted that they would all be either gulfed or ploughed.","ref":"1863, Henry Kingsley, Austin Elliot, page 123:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[78,86]],"id":"14bad1da-d343-43d4-9b2f-b691a96913b6"},{"text":"You see, Miss Dodd, an university examination consists of several items: neglect but one, and Crichton himself would be ploughed; because brilliancy in your other papers is not allowed to count; that is how the most distinguished man of our day got ploughed for Smalls.","ref":"1863, Charles Reade, Hard Cash:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[120,128],[249,257]],"id":"b8122aef-20e6-49d4-a675-ddc46719a49f"},{"text":"I knew one of that lot at Corpus; in fact, we were crammed by the same tutor for \"smalls,\" and both got ploughed.","ref":"1895, Roger Pocock, The Rules of the Game:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[104,112]],"id":"ef29bb6f-a234-4e36-8072-ef025abf7b25"}],"categories":["British English","English student slang","English terms with obsolete senses","English terms with quotations","English transitive verbs","en:Universities"],"links":[["university","university"],["fail","fail"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"flunk"},{"word":"pluck"},{"word":"spin"}],"raw_glosses":["(UK, university slang, obsolete, transitive) To fail (a student)."],"qualifier":"university slang","glosses":["To fail (a student)."],"tags":["UK","obsolete","transitive"],"id":"22a6d026-84f4-4db7-b35a-8579377d5a6f"},{"examples":[{"text":"I love just getting ploughed face down on my bed.","bold_text_offsets":[[20,28]],"type":"example","id":"e039c956-167b-44fc-8a2f-40588475ae2a"},{"text":"If you don't use rubbers when you're plowing someone's throat, don't bother answering this ad.","ref":"1990 February 11, “Working Up An Appetite (personal advertisement)”, in Gay Community News, volume 17, number 30, page 13:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[37,44]],"id":"25b020ac-366b-4b74-8a55-c87eeb3fda89"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs","English vulgarities"],"links":[["penetrate","penetrate"],["vigorous","vigorous"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"get up in"},{"word":"pound"},{"word":"Formal terms","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"bed","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"coit","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"coitize","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"dight","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"enjoy","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"feague","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"go in unto","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"go to bed with","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"have","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"know","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["archaic"]},{"word":"lie by","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"lie with","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"love","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["euphemistic"]},{"word":"mount","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"occupy","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"penetrate","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"season","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"seduce","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["euphemistic"]},{"word":"sleep with","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"take","source":"Thesauru