e one's wicked way with","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"hit","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"hump","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"jape","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"jump","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"jump someone's bones","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"knob","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["UK"]},{"word":"knock","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"knock off","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"know someone in the biblical sense","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"lay","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"love up","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"make","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"nail","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"plough","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"plug","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"poke","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"pork","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"pound","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"prig","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"pump","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["UK","obsolete"]},{"word":"ravage","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"ream","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"rock","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["euphemistic"]},{"word":"roger","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"root","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["Australia"]},{"word":"run through","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["idiomatic"]},{"word":"rut","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["rare"]},{"word":"sard","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"schlong","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"screw","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"see to","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["UK"]},{"word":"service","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"shaft","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"shag","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"shelve","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["Wales"]},{"word":"slay","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"slip it to","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"smash","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"smush","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["US","dialectal"]},{"word":"stallionize","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"swive","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"tap","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"tup","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"},{"word":"wap","source":"Thesaurus:copulate with"}],"raw_glosses":["(transitive, vulgar) To sexually penetrate, typically in a vigorous manner."],"glosses":["To sexually penetrate, typically in a vigorous manner."],"tags":["transitive","vulgar"],"id":"714fdf4a-d5b4-4e71-818a-198ce469d927"}],"related":[{"word":"disc"},{"word":"furrow"},{"word":"harrow"},{"word":"rake"},{"word":"till"},{"word":"yoke"}],"synonyms":[]},"ploy":{"id":"51974c10-7347-4d3e-bcce-9a722dae5618","word":"ploy","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"strategy, tactic","tags":["masculine"],"word":"estratagema"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Near-synonyms: ruse, stratagem, device","id":"a66e6766-3655-468a-82a4-9dfb006f9aa5"},{"text":"The free T-shirt is really a ploy to get you inside to see their sales pitch.","bold_text_offsets":[[29,33]],"type":"example","id":"90358201-559e-4514-a8a1-081aa3743638"},{"text":"'Bide here,' he says, 'and boil the wine till I return. This is a ploy of my own on which no man follows me.' And there was that in his face, as he spoke, which chilled the wildest, and left them well content to keep to the good claret and the saft seat, and let the daft laird go his own ways.","ref":"1902, John Buchan, The Outgoing of the Tide:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[66,70]],"id":"ed404f48-c39d-4280-8691-b816b49ccbda"},{"text":"Private-equity nabobs bristle at being dubbed mere financiers.[…]Much of their pleading is public-relations bluster. Clever financial ploys are what have made billionaires of the industry’s veterans. “Operational improvement” in a portfolio company has often meant little more than promising colossal bonuses to sitting chief executives if they meet ambitious growth targets. That model is still prevalent today.","ref":"2013 June 22, “Engineers of a different kind”, in The Economist, volume 407, number 8841, page 70:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[134,139]],"id":"f5ac13a6-a479-465d-bb49-5be6380aa0bf"}],"links":[["tactic","tactic"],["strategy","strategy"],["scheme","scheme"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"ruse"},{"word":"stratagem"},{"word":"device"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","Quotation templates to be cleaned"],"glosses":["A tactic, strategy, or scheme."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"1289eb06-9f39-4385-ad03-c9a0f5248e1d"},{"categories":["British English","English dialectal terms","Scottish English"],"links":[["Sport","sport"],["frolic","frolic"]],"raw_glosses":["(UK, Scotland, dialect) Sport; frolic."],"glosses":["Sport; frolic."],"tags":["Scotland","UK","countable","dialectal","uncountable"],"id":"8396f224-c92e-4236-b5ab-7b5d769ce95a"},{"categories":["English terms with obsolete senses"],"links":[["Employment","employment"]],"raw_glosses":["(obsolete) Employment."],"glosses":["Employment."],"tags":["countable","obsolete","uncountable"],"id":"c1f2d5b0-7ab4-459e-abac-800d89dd85bf"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"plonk":{"id":"80b7f1c3-eb97-49a2-a66e-8deb2e3fa4f0","word":"plonk","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"cheap or inferior wine","word":"zurrapa"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"The third category of wines is highly unattractive as these may only be sold as generic wines (white, red or rosé), without reference to any geographical location. Only surplus plonk and cooking wine would aspire to fall in this segment, which can be blended with any other wine - to any extent.","ref":"1998, Pierre Spahni, Swiss Wine Market Report, page 95:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[177,182]],"id":"2f89f13e-34f9-4c3c-b8fe-8f74025efda0"},{"text":"Diesel took a large swallow out of the glass of red wine. He spluttered, choked, and spilled wine down one leg of his fawn colored pants. “My God,” he gasped, when he could speak. “What is that crap?”\n“Why cheap red wine,” Ford displayed the label. “You know. Plonk.”","ref":"2003, Joan del Monte, Plonk Goes the Weasel, page 201:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[260,265]],"id":"997807c9-2e5a-42e8-b7fa-00b5a15daeaf"},{"text":"2011, Charles Spence, Maya U. Shankar, Heston Blumenthal, Chapter 11: ‘Sound Bites’: Auditory Contributions to the Perceeption and Consumption of Food and Drink, Francesca Bacci, David Melcher (editors), Art and the Senses, page 229,\nGiven the results reported in this chapter, one obvious solution to the ‘plonk paradox’ (why cheap wine tastes good on holiday but terrible at home) would be to try and recapture some of these sensory impressions in one′s own living room, in order to enhance the flavour/pleasantness of the wine-drinking experience (and turn that horrible tasting wine into something that tastes really rather nice), and to elucidate the respective contributions of contextual effects on hedonic ratings.","id":"6a309a3e-863d-48ed-b25b-48901a37eccc"}],"senseid":["en:wine"],"categories":["British English","Commonwealth English","English informal terms","English terms with quotations","English uncountable nouns","Irish English"],"links":[["Cheap","cheap"],["inferior","inferior"],["everyday","everyday"],["wine","wine"]],"raw_glosses":["(uncountable, UK, Ireland, Commonwealth, informal) Cheap or inferior everyday wine."],"glosses":["Cheap or inferior everyday wine."],"tags":["Commonwealth","Ireland","UK","informal","uncountable"],"id":"07d0abc3-b9df-45e3-b9b6-27b9799f1049"},{"topics":["government","military","politics","war"],"categories":["English slang","English terms with historical senses","en:Military"],"links":[["military","military"],["AC Plonk","AC Plonk"]],"raw_glosses":["(military, slang, historical) AC Plonk"],"glosses":["AC Plonk"],"tags":["historical","slang","uncountable"],"id":"6a6a3b17-275c-4eca-ac68-2ff5b006c706"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"plot twist":{"id":"f92e41b9-edc4-4d7b-95e2-f0a95b9750f2","word":"plot twist","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"change in the direction or expected outcome of the plot","tags":["feminine"],"word":"reviravolta"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"change in the direction or expected outcome of the plot","tags":["masculine"],"word":"plot twist"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"It was daring of Lena Dunham, the show’s writer and creator, to introduce this particular plot twist. Assisted suicide is one of the subjects that American television shows steadily avoid.","ref":"2014 March 27, Robin Marantz Henig, “Fictional Plotlines and Real Assisted Suicide”, in The Atlantic:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[90,100]],"id":"5ea3802e-251f-40bf-b747-e29112240aea"}],"links":[["change","change"],["direction","direction"],["expected","expected"],["outcome","outcome"],["plot","plot"],["film","film"],["novel","novel"],["narrative","narrative"],["work","work"]],"categories":["English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English multiword terms","English nouns","English terms with quotations","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Terms with Dutch translations","Terms with Estonian translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Greek translations","Terms with Italian translations","Terms with Japanese translations","Terms with